4. ANDROID ACCESSIBILITY SERVICE — THE MAIN CONTROL ENGINE

Implement a properly designed Android AccessibilityService.

This is a core requirement for controlling third-party app interfaces.

Subject to Android restrictions and user-granted permissions, it should support:

- Reading accessible UI element text.
- Identifying visible buttons.
- Identifying text fields.
- Identifying menus and navigation elements.
- Clicking accessible UI elements.
- Scrolling lists and pages.
- Entering text into supported text fields.
- Performing supported long-press actions.
- Navigating backward.
- Detecting UI changes.
- Waiting for an interface to load.
- Reporting the result to the Action Executor.

Accessibility requirements

- Create a dedicated AccessibilityService implementation.
- Add the correct service declaration and metadata.
- Configure only the required accessibility capabilities.
- Provide a clear in-app explanation of why this permission is needed.
- Add a user-directed button that opens Android Accessibility Settings.
- Detect whether the service is enabled.
- Display its actual permission status in the application.
- Provide a clear setup guide for enabling the service.
- Handle service disconnection and reconnection gracefully.
- Do not falsely display that the service is enabled when it is not.

The user must manually enable the AccessibilityService in Android Settings.

Do not attempt to bypass Android permission screens.

Important limitations

AccessibilityService cannot guarantee control over every app or every screen.

Secure windows, protected content, custom-rendered interfaces, restricted applications, and Android system limitations may prevent some actions.

If a target element cannot be accessed:

1. Try a supported alternative, such as an Intent or deep link.
2. Use the existing screen-sharing visual context when appropriate.
3. Ask the user for help if the operation remains unavailable.
4. Clearly explain the limitation.

Never claim universal control over all Android applications.

